<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kashag</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kashag was the governing council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> government, formally established in 1751 after reforms by the Qing and the Seventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. It normally had four ministers, the kalons, usually one monk and three lay officials, who answered to the Dalai Lama or regent. The name was later used for the cabinet of the Tibetan government in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashag.jpg" alt="Kashag" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Schäfer · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_135-S-13-11-31,_Tibetexpedition,_Minister.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A council had first been created in 1721. Its lasting form was set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> in 1751 in the 13-Article Ordinance for the More Effective Governing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, when the Tibetan government was reorganised after the riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> of the previous year. The Qing court abolished the post of Desi, or regent, and the civil administration passed to the council; the court wanted the 7th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> to hold both religious and administrative rule while it strengthened the position of its High Commissioners. Each member held the title of Kalön, sought appointment from the Qing court and received a certificate of appointment from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The council decided government affairs collectively and passed its opinions to the first minister, who put them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> and, under the Qing, to the Amban for a final decision. Secular matters had to go through it, formal orders needed its seal, and its right to recommend executive officials, governors and district commissioners gave it much power. It had no authority over monks, religion or the monasteries, which belonged to the chigyab khembo and the Yigtsang Office. Below it stood ministries for political, military, economic, judicial, foreign, financial and educational affairs.</p><p>Surkhang Shape estimated that in a six-day week in the 1940s about 100 pieces of business, some 30 percent of the whole, went on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>; the council settled the rest. Its weight rose under regencies and fell away under strong Dalai Lamas. Of the 41 ministers between 1900 and 1959, 12 were monks and 29 lay officials, most of the lay members high-status aristocrats. From 1913 to 1951 the council also called three kinds of National Assembly, made up of officials and abbots of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> monasteries for consultation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>, premier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, formally announced the dissolution of the Kashag on 28 March 1959. On 29 April 1959 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> re-established it in exile, and under the Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> of 1963 he appointed all its ministers. The Charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> in Exile of 1991 made it a Chief Kalon and seven Kalons. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> gave up political authority in March 2011, the charter was revised in May 2011 so that the Kashag consists of the Sikyong and no more than seven Kalons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kashag” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tibet was governed by the Ganden Phodrang, the government of the Dalai Lamas, with the <b>Kashag</b> as its council of ministers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Tibet the Dalai Lama headed a government, the Ganden Phodrang, in which a council of ministers, the <b>Kashag</b>, and parallel ranks of monk and lay officials administered the country.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>Tibet was governed by the Dalai Lamas or their regents with a council of ministers (the <b>Kashag</b>, formalised in 1751) and a dual service of monk and lay officials.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li></ul></section>
